;;; DRAFT -- span!
;;; Edited 2026-08-03 by Priya.
;;; AUTHORED, not model-seeded. The drafting batch
;;; (scratch/pairs/draft_records.py, see draft_run.log) skipped
;;; this verb because an edited draft already existed, so no raw
;;; model output for it survives. Claiming model provenance here
;;; would be provenance nobody can check.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/span-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "span!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(span! pred lst) -> two values: (prefix rest)"
  :summary "The longest leading run satisfying pred, and everything after it, as two values, cut out of lst's own cells."
  :explanation "span! is to split-at! what take-while! is to take!: the cut point is discovered by a predicate instead of supplied as an index. It is the verb that makes take-while! usable, because it returns the part take-while! throws away. The suite checks both returned lists at list.sld:534-543 and requires every cell of both to have come from the argument -- nothing is allocated. The bang is a licence to destroy. After the call the cells that made up the argument have been re-used, so the variable you passed in no longer names what it named. Bind the result and read only that. The relationship worth memorising is that span! and break! are the same verb with the predicate inverted, and that (span! pred lst) is exactly (take-while! pred lst) paired with the drop-while the caller could not have taken afterwards."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(define xs (list 2 18 3 10 22 9))\n(let-values (((run rest) (span! even? xs)))\n  (display (list 'run run 'rest rest))\n  (newline))"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; both values are carved from xs; the run is xs cut short.\n(define xs (list 2 18 3 10))\n(let-values (((run rest) (span! even? xs)))\n  (display (list 'run-is-xs (eq? run xs) 'rest rest))\n  (newline))"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; span! and break! are one verb under two names for the predicate.\n(define a (list 2 4 5 6))\n(define b (list 2 4 5 6))\n(let-values (((ra sa) (span! even? a)) ((rb sb) (break! odd? b)))\n  (display (list 'span ra sa 'break rb sb))\n  (newline))"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The argument is destroyed when the run is non-empty. list.sld:534-543 asserts every cell of both results came from the argument."
             "When the first element fails the predicate the run is empty, nothing is written, and the second value is the argument itself."
             "It returns TWO values; bind them with let-values."
             "pred stops being called at the first failure. A predicate with side effects will not see the remainder.")
  :drawbacks ("Like take-while!, whether anything was destroyed depends on the data, so the hazard is intermittent."
             "Holding both halves keeps the whole original reachable, so the memory saving over span is smaller than it looks."
             "The pred-inverted equivalence with break! means two names for one behaviour, and readers must check which one they are looking at.")
  :usecases ("Consuming a leading run off a self-owned worklist while keeping the remainder to loop on."
             "Tokenising a character list in place, run by run."
             "The safe replacement for take-while! whenever the tail is still needed.")
  :related ("span"
             "break!"
             "take-while!"
             "drop-while"
             "split-at!")
  :learn (:concept "Predicate-found cut points that return both sides"
          :prerequisites ("span" "take-while!" "split-at!" "let-values")
          :progression "Learn span, then take-while!, then span! -- the third is the first two reconciled, and the reconciliation is the point.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 linear-update procedures; conformance evidence scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:534-543; signature derived from the span record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat. Drafted 2026-08-03; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 2 18 3 10))\n(let-values (((run rest) (span! even? xs)))\n  (display (list run rest))\n  (newline))"
     :math-program "(define a (list 2 18 3 10))\n(define b (list 2 18 3 10))\n(let-values (((ra sa) (span even? a)) ((rb sb) (span! even? b)))\n  (display (list (equal? ra rb) (equal? sa sb)))\n  (newline))"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 3 4))\n(let-values (((run rest) (span! even? xs)))\n  (display (list 'empty-run (null? run) 'rest-is-argument (eq? rest xs)))\n  (newline))"
     :problem "The operator is draining a worklist a run at a time and needs both the run and what is left. Candidates: span (both halves fresh, source intact), take-while plus drop-while (two traversals, and the predicate runs twice), or span! (one traversal, one write, source destroyed). take-while! is not a candidate at all here, because it discards the remainder the loop needs."
     :explanation "eng: the run comes back and so does the rest, which is what lets the loop continue. math: both values agree with span for every predicate and list. cs: the cells are the argument's, so the two halves are views onto one destroyed original rather than independent objects."
     :meta "The candidate that gets eliminated first is the informative one: take-while! loses the tail, and a loop cannot be built on a verb that loses the tail.")
    (:eng "Choose the verb that returns what the loop needs next, not the one that reads shortest."
     :math "span computes take-while and drop-while in one pass; the predicate is evaluated once per element."
     :cs "Two traversals with a side-effecting predicate is a correctness bug, not just a slow path.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Splitting a sequence at the first element that fails a test, keeping both sides."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(let-values (((r s) (span! even? (list 2 4 5 6))))\n  (display (list r s))\n  (newline))")
             (:domain "a queue drained until the first blocked job" :code "(define q (list 'ready 'ready 'blocked 'ready))\n(let-values (((served waiting) (span! (lambda (j) (eq? j 'ready)) q)))\n  (display (list 'served served 'waiting waiting))\n  (newline))")
             (:domain "a line of text split at the first non-digit" :code "(define chars (list #\\4 #\\2 #\\x #\\7))\n(let-values (((digits rest) (span! char-numeric? chars)))\n  (display (list 'digits digits 'rest rest))\n  (newline))")
     )
     :explanation "In list, the split lands at the first failure. A queue drained until the first blocked job stops there and the blocked job is still at the head of what remains, ready for a different policy. A tokeniser splitting at the first non-digit does the same and hands the remainder to the next rule. All three share a boundary the data chooses and a remainder that is still wanted."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a scan stops on a condition and the unscanned part continues to another stage.")
    (:eng "A stop rule that discards the remainder is a scan; one that returns it is a stage in a pipeline."
     :math "The failing element belongs to the second part, which is what makes repeated spanning terminate."
     :cs "This is the classic lexer step: match a run, hand the rest to the next rule.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 2 18 3))\n(let-values (((r s) (span! even? xs)))\n  (display (list 'run r 'rest s))\n  (newline))"
     :math-program "(define (span-laws? p xs)\n  (let-values (((r s) (span p xs)))\n    (and (every p r)\n         (or (null? s) (not (p (car s))))\n         (equal? (append r s) xs))))\n(display (list (span-laws? even? (list 2 4 5 6)) (span-laws? even? (list 1 2)) (span-laws? even? (list 2 4))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define a (list 2 4 5))\n(define b (list 2 4 5))\n(let-values (((ra sa) (span! even? a)) ((rb sb) (break! odd? b)))\n  (display (list 'same-run (equal? ra rb) 'same-rest (equal? sa sb)))\n  (newline))"
     :claim "span! returns two values: the maximal prefix of lst all of whose elements satisfy pred, and the remainder beginning with the first element that does not. Their append equals the original contents, and both are built from lst's own cells. It agrees value-for-value with span, and with break! under a negated predicate."
     :invariant "forall pred, proper lst. (r, s) = span!(pred, lst) satisfies every(pred, r) and (s = () or not pred(car(s))) and equal?(append(r, s), lst-as-it-was) and span!(pred, lst) = break!(not-pred, lst)"
     :meta "The pattern is a predicate-located partition. What would falsify the claim: a remainder whose head satisfies pred (not maximal), an append that does not reconstruct the original, or disagreement with break! under negation.")
    (:eng "Three assertions carry this: all of the run passes, the head of the rest fails, and the two rejoin."
     :math "Maximality plus reconstruction pins the answer uniquely; either alone admits wrong splits."
     :cs "The span/break duality is worth a test of its own, because an implementation that gets one right can still get the other inverted.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (drain-ready q)\n  (let-values (((served waiting) (span (lambda (j) (eq? j 'ready)) q)))\n    (list 'served served 'waiting waiting)))\n(display (drain-ready (list 'ready 'ready 'blocked)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (runs p xs)\n  (if (null? xs)\n      (list)\n      (let-values (((r s) (span p xs)))\n        (if (null? r)\n            (cons (list (car xs)) (runs p (cdr xs)))\n            (cons r (runs p s))))))\n(display (runs even? (list 2 4 5 6 8)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (tokenise chars)\n  (if (null? chars)\n      (list)\n      (let-values (((digits rest) (span char-numeric? chars)))\n        (if (null? digits)\n            (cons (car chars) (tokenise (cdr chars)))\n            (cons digits (tokenise rest))))))\n(display (tokenise (list #\\1 #\\2 #\\x #\\3)))\n(newline)"
     :base "span! cuts a list at the first element failing a predicate and returns both parts."
     :emergent "eng: a drain step that a scheduler can call in a loop. math: repeated spanning is run-length segmentation, the thing take-while! could not express -- and note the empty-run branch, which is what stops the recursion from spinning. cs: a tokeniser is that segmentation with the predicate changed; shown pure so the recursion is readable, and destructive only if the character list is genuinely single-owner."
     :meta "The family is repeated segmentation. What emerges is the same rule split-at! demonstrated: a linear-update verb is loopable exactly when it returns its own remainder.")
    (:eng "The empty-run guard is not defensive coding, it is what makes the loop terminate."
     :math "Run-length segmentation is repeated maximal-prefix extraction; the remainder is the recursion variable."
     :cs "Every lexer is this shape, which is why the verb that returns the remainder is the one that got standardised.")
  )
)
